  <static seed="1877" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/FractionApplication/FractionAdditionWordProblems10.pg">
    <statement><p>Benjamin walked <m>{{\frac{3}{11}}}</m> of a mile in the morning, and then walked <m>{{\frac{1}{6}}}</m> of a mile in the afternoon. How far did Benjamin walk altogether?</p>
<p>Benjamin walked a total of <fillin characters="11" name="AnSwEr0001"/> of a mile.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\frac{29}{66}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>This is an addition problem.</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
\begin{aligned}[t]
   {{\frac{3}{11}}} + {{\frac{1}{6}}} 
      \amp = \frac{3 \cdot 6}{11 \cdot 6}+\frac{1 \cdot 11}{6 \cdot 11} \\
      \amp = \frac{18}{66} + \frac{11}{66} \\
      \amp = \frac{18+11}{66} \\
      
      \amp = {{\frac{29}{66}}}
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>Benjamin walked a total of <m>{{\frac{29}{66}}}</m> miles altogether.</p>
</solution>
